We had postgres user getting removed after the system rebbot. Please perform the below steps to keep the DB running.


Step 1: Fix the postgres User & Group
Run these commands to force the system to recognize the correct user and group for the database:

# 1. Check what the 'postgres' group GID actually is
grep postgres /etc/group

# 2. If it is NOT 26, change it to 26 (to match your database files)
groupmod -g 26 postgres

# 3. Now create the user (this should work now that group 26 is aligned)
useradd -u 26 -g 26 -d /var/lib/pgsql -s /bin/bash postgres

# 4. Verify - this MUST return UID 26 and GID 26
id postgres

Step 2: Fix Permissions & Runtime Directory
Postgres needs its socket directory in /run, which is always deleted on reboot.


# 1. Fix ownership of the data files
chown -R postgres:postgres /var/lib/pgsql/

# 2. Create the missing runtime directory
mkdir -p /run/postgresql
chown postgres:postgres /run/postgresql
chmod 775 /run/postgresql

# 3. Clean up any stale lock files from the crash/reboot
rm -f /var/lib/pgsql/15/data/postmaster.pid

Step 3: Start Services
# 1. Start Database
systemctl start postgresql-15

# 2. Check Database Status (Must be active/running)
systemctl status postgresql-15

# 3. Start FIM Backend
pkill -9 -f uvicorn
cd /opt/fim
source venv/bin/activate
nohup uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 > /opt/fim/logs/uvicorn.log 2>&1 &

Step 4: Prevent this from happening on next reboot
To ensure the postgres user and /run directory stay fixed after the next reboot, we need to make the configuration permanent.

1. Fix the Runtime Directory permanently:
echo "d /run/postgresql 0775 postgres postgres -" > /etc/tmpfiles.d/postgresql.conf

2. Ensure the FIM Agent and Backend start on boot:
If you don't have them in systemd yet, you should. But for now, check if the Agent is running (it likely stopped too):


# Start Agent on test06
cd /opt/fim/agent
nohup python3 fim_agent.py --config /opt/fim/agent/config/agent_config.yaml > logs/fim_agent.log 2>&1 &
